parser_ext/README

Postgres Parser Extension
=========================

This is a skeleton for the experimental Postgres parser extension module.

In order to use it:

1. Run bootstrap.sh specifying Postgres source and install path and the module
output path. Note that access to the Postgres source tree is required. You may
want to update module name and description in the generated Makefile and
parser_ext.c

   PGSRC=/path/to/postgres/   \
   PGSQL=/path/to/pgsql/      \
   OUTDIR=/path/to/parser_ext \
   ./bootstrap.sh

2. In the generated module source tree update the my_kwlist.h and my_gram.y as
usual. Beware that the whole parser will be replaced, so make sure not to break
the core syntax.

3. Compile the module by running make.

4. Load the module as a shared library file.

   LOAD '/path/to/module/parser_ext.so'

You may find an example of the parser extension module in the lowcard_ext.

At present, only one parser extension can be registered per session. As sessions
may load different sets of modules, each session may end up using a different
parser.

Design
------

We rely on the well-established hooks system to replace the parser function and
scanner token tables. The parser function is generated from the modified
grammar, and token tables are generated from the modified kwlist.h. Because of
this any changes to either of them must be limited to additions.

Access to the Postgres source tree is required to copy the original grammar and
keyword list, as well as helper Perl scripts. It would be possible to add
keywords truly dynamically (without replacing scanner token tables, for example
add_keyword("low")) by changing the perfect hashing function to a dynamic one:
current hash function is generated at the compile time.

Note that only parser will be aware of the new keywords, as (internal) kwlist.h
is included in several other places. As of Postgres 18 this affects
pg_get_keywords() function, psql's tab-completion, deparsing of internal queries
and representations (e.g. pg_get_viewdef()) and frontend utilities such as
pg_dump.

Future ideas
------------

* Add hooks for more processing functions from the src/backed/parser.

* Add support for the syntax sugar: construct the statement in-place and append
it to the "after list" of statements.

* Support multiple parsers per session.


